users`.** `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The planner has to read every row and compute `lower()` on each one to find the match. This is the most expensive problem here, and it grows linearly with the size of `users`.\n\n2. **Full table scan on `orders`, or a hash join that reads all of it.** This depends on whether `orders.user_id` is indexed. If it isn't, each matching user triggers a scan of `orders`. Alternatively, the planner builds a hash join that reads the whole table. Foreign keys are not indexed automatically in Postgres, or in MySQL/InnoDB when the FK isn't declared.\n\n3. **Wide row reads from `SELECT *`.** This pulls every column from both tables, including any large text or JSON columns. It prevents index-only scans, and it duplicates column names such as `id` and `created_at` across the two tables, which can break or confuse clients.\n\n## Proposed fixes\n\n**Index the lookup (pick one):**\n\n```sql\n-- Option A: expression index (Postgres)\nCREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- Option B: normalize at write time, then use a plain index\n-- Store email lowercased (or use citext in Postgres), then:\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_users_email ON users (email);\n-- and query: WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'\n```\n\nOption B is preferable if you can enforce lowercase on write. It's a smaller index, it enforces uniqueness, and the query stays simple. Option A works without changing data. The query has to keep using exactly `lower(email)` to match the index. On MySQL, a case-insensitive collation on `email` makes `lower()` unnecessary.\n\n**Index the join:**\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\n\n**Rewrite to select only what's needed:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\nThe columns above are placeholders. Replace them with what the caller actually uses. If you only need orders for one user, you can add an `ORDER BY` and `LIMIT`. In that case, make the index `(user_id, created_at)` so the sort is served by the index.\n\n## Expected plan after the changes\n\nAn index scan on `users` returns one row. A nested-loop index scan on `orders` then uses `idx_orders_user_id`. I haven't run `EXPLAIN` because there's no schema or database in this workspace. Check the plan with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on real data to confirm the index scans.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}